<Wiki
  title="puv install"
  description="Install exactly puv.lock, or add a package when you pass one."
>
  <p class="kicker">Wiki</p>
  <h1>install</h1>
  <p>
    <code>puv install</code> is two commands, chosen by whether you pass a package. This page is
    the project command. The binary itself is installed from
    <a href="/wiki/install">Install</a>.
  </p>

  <h2>No arguments</h2>
  <pre class="block">puv install</pre>
  <p>
    This installs exactly what <code>puv.lock</code> says. It does not resolve. It is the same
    install as <a href="/wiki/commands/sync"><code>puv sync</code></a>.
  </p>
  <p>
    If <code>puv.toml</code> drifted from the lock, the command stops and tells you to run
    <a href="/wiki/commands/lock"><code>puv lock</code></a>. It will not paper over the drift by
    resolving on its own.
  </p>
  <pre class="block"># puv.toml gained a dependency the lock does not know
puv install
# stops: run puv lock
puv lock
puv install</pre>

  <h2>With a package</h2>
  <pre class="block">puv install symfony/console
puv install symfony/console:^7.0</pre>
  <p>
    A package argument is an alias of <a href="/wiki/commands/add"><code>puv add</code></a>. It
    records the dependency, resolves, writes the lock, and installs. A constraint after the colon
    is written into the manifest.
  </p>
  <p>
    A download prints a progress line on stderr. A cached archive stays quiet. Installed bytes
    are linked from <code>.puv/deps/</code> into <code>~/.cache/puv/</code>.
  </p>
</Wiki>
